tel 1.0

base soundness
import soundness

info   the umbrella package: one `import soundness.*` reaches every Soundness library
detail
    _Soundness_ is a family of well over a hundred libraries, each in a package of its own
    (`gossamer` for text, `galilei` for the filesystem, `jacinta` for JSON, …), and
    `soundness` is the package which gathers them. A program writes one wildcard import and
    then names whatever it needs, from any library, without knowing which library it came
    from:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import systems.javaBaseSystem

    val greeting: Text = t"Hello, world".upper
    val xs: List[Int] = List(3, 1, 2)
    val user: Text = System.properties.user.name()
    ```

    The package holds almost nothing of its own. Every library has, in each of its
    components, a file named `soundness_<library>_<component>.scala` which `export`s that
    component's public names into `package soundness`, so the umbrella grows with the
    components on the classpath: a program that depends on `galilei.jvm` sees galilei's
    filesystem backends in it, and one that does not, does not. These forwarders are
    documented where they are defined, on each library's own page — `Classloader` on
    hellenism's, `Json` on jacinta's — and not here. This page documents only what the
    umbrella *declares* itself: a handful of hand-written forwarders which an `export`
    cannot express, the collection aliases, two annotation types, the catch-all
    `explainMissingContext`, and the `repackage` entry point.

    **Choice packages.** A given which selects an implementation — an error-handling
    strategy, a classloader, a codepage, a log format, a filesystem backend — lives in a
    small package named for its role: `strategies`, `classloaders`, `codepages`,
    `logFormats`, `filesystemBackends`. The umbrella mirrors each as a `package` of the same
    name, and blocks of the same name from different libraries merge, so
    `soundness.construables` holds the `Construable` givens of jacinta, xylophone,
    honeycomb, caesura and a dozen others, and `soundness.wasiApis` those of every library
    with a WASI backend. The path through the umbrella and the path through the library are
    the same given: `import soundness.monthFormats.englishMonths` and `import
    aviation.monthFormats.englishMonths` are interchangeable.

    A choice-package given is always **imported by name**, because `import strategies.*`
    does not import givens: Scala's wildcard selects only ordinary members, and a given
    needs its own name or a `given` selector. And a given imported into the lexical scope
    outranks any given in a companion object, which is what lets a choice package override
    a library's default. `import soundness.*` itself therefore brings no choice into scope —
    with one deliberate exception, `explainMissingContext`, which is an old-style `implicit
    def` precisely so that the wildcard does include it.

    **Names left out.** Types and terms live in separate namespaces, so a type `via` and a
    method `via` coexist; but two libraries which export one *term* (or one *type*) into
    `package soundness` do not overload — the compiler keeps one and silently drops the
    other. Each such clash has been ruled on, and the loser is reached through its own
    package, where it keeps its name:

    ```tabular
    column  Name
    column  In the umbrella
    column  Reach the other as
    row  `Channel`  perihelion's WebSocket  `anticipation.Channel` (pixel channels)
    row  `Completion`, `Completions`  exoskeleton's  `harlequin.Completion`, `prophesy.Completion`
    row  `Fragment`  honeycomb's  `harlequin.Fragment`
    row  `Descriptor`  embarcadero's OCI  `hallucination.Descriptor`
    row  `Diagnostic`  harlequin's  `frontier.Diagnostic`
    row  `Message`  fulminate's  `perihelion.Websocket.Message` (a WebSocket frame)
    row  `Tag`  honeycomb's HTML tag  `probably.Tag`, `xylophone.Tag`
    row  `Attributive`, `Renderable`  honeycomb's  `xylophone.Attributive`, `xylophone.Renderable`
    row  `Universe`, `Classfile`  anthology's, mandible's  `degustation.Universe`, `degustation.Classfile`
    row  `as`  distillate's decoding  `scintillate.as` (a request body)
    row  `teletype`  escapade's rendering  `delicious.teletype`
    row  `Kelvin(…)` as an `apply`  rudiments' deindexing `apply`  `import quantitative.*`
    ```

    A few names are absent for a different reason: an `export` cannot forward them
    faithfully. rudiments' `Scribe`/`scribe` and `Surveyor`/`survey`, and zephyrine's
    `Region` and `Slate`, are reached from `rudiments` and `zephyrine` directly; perihelion's
    `wsClient` and `wsSessional` are imported by name from `perihelion`. A definition
    deliberately kept out of the umbrella carries the `unexported` annotation in its source,
    and each library's page says which of its names are not exported. The MathML element
    types sit inside `Mathml` rather than at the top level, so `import soundness.Mathml.*`
    gives the bare element names where MathML is written by hand.

    One shadowing is worth knowing: ambience's `System` is exported, so under `import
    soundness.*` a bare `System` is the Soundness one, and the JDK's must be written
    `java.lang.System` (for `System.nanoTime`, `System.arraycopy`, …).

    **Why some forwarders are hand-written.** A synthesized `export` forwarder is a plain
    method, and some definitions do not survive the trip, so the umbrella re-declares them,
    each delegating to its library's original:

    - an `export` of an *overloaded* extension forwards only one overload, so `thru`, `till`,
      `tabulate` and the interval `each` are declared here, every overload in one
      compilation unit;
    - an extension whose typeclass evidence is a dependent leading `using` clause — the
      deindexing group `apply`, `at`, `defines`, `confine`, `pick`, `unsafeAttested`, with
      `define`, `omit`, `keep` and `skip` — needs that clause so that a failed summon
      discards the candidate, and a forwarder cannot carry it;
    - a forwarder loses a capture-annotated refinement, so zephyrine's `via` and `accepting`,
      contingency's `throwUnsafely` and scintillate's four `httpServers` givens are written
      out with their capture sets;
    - an `export` of an object is a `def`, an unstable prefix through which an opaque type is
      transparent, so the collection companions are forwarded as stable `val`s beside
      parameterised type aliases (`List`, `Sequence`, `Set`, `Map`, `Chain`);
    - an `export` of a case class brings its constructor proxy, a term, so `assign` and
      `attribute` are type aliases alone, leaving the term names to anamnesis and galilei.

    **How the documentation is organised.** Each package has its own page, named for it;
    a name reached through `soundness` is documented on the page of the package which
    defines it, and each library's page says which of its components contribute what,
    which choice packages it mirrors into the umbrella, and which givens are imported by
    name. This page has an entry for each declaration of the umbrella's own, and for the
    two choice packages whose givens are declared here rather than exported.

refer soundness.explainMissingContext
refer soundness.strategies
refer soundness.List
refer proscenium.unexported

entry .List   the opaque linked list `proscenium.List`, with its companion as a stable value
    `type List[+element] = proscenium.List[element]` and `val List = proscenium.List`: the
    Soundness list, documented on proscenium's page, as the umbrella names it. The type is
    a parameterised alias, since a bare alias of an opaque constructor is not seen through
    (`::`, `CanEqual` and the extension methods would treat it as a different `List`); and
    the companion is a `val` rather than an `export`ed `def`, since a companion member
    selected through a `def` prefix sees the opaque type's right-hand side and exposes the
    standard library's whole API. `Nil`, `::` and `#::` are forwarded beside it.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val xs: List[Int] = List(1, 2, 3)
    xs.map(_ + 1)
    ```

    A list built from a standard-library collection is `List.from(xs)`, not `xs.to[List]`,
    which on a stdlib receiver reaches Scala's own `to` and fails for want of a factory.

entry .Sequence   the opaque indexed sequence `proscenium.Sequence`, with its companion as a stable value
    `type Sequence[+element] = proscenium.Sequence[element]` and `val Sequence =
    proscenium.Sequence`: an immutable `Vector` behind an opaque type, as proscenium's page
    describes it. Forwarded as a parameterised alias and a stable `val` for the reasons
    `List` is.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val xs = Sequence(10, 20, 30)
    xs.pick(Sec) { i => xs(i) + 1 }   // 21
    ```

entry .Set   the opaque hash set `proscenium.Set`, with its companion as a stable value
    `type Set[element] = proscenium.Set[element]` and `val Set = proscenium.Set`, forwarded
    as an alias and a stable `val` for the reasons `List` is. A `Set` is `Reshapable` but has
    no stable order, so it does not `sort`.

entry .Map   the opaque hash map `proscenium.Map`, with its companion as a stable value
    `type Map[key, +value] = proscenium.Map[key, value]` and `val Map = proscenium.Map`,
    forwarded as an alias and a stable `val` for the reasons `List` is. `map.confine(key)`,
    `map(key)`, `map.define(key, value)` and `map.omit(key)` are the umbrella's deindexing
    extensions.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val ages = Map(t"Hamlet" -> 30)
    ages.confine(t"Hamlet").let(ages(_))   // 30
    ```

entry .Chain   the opaque lazy sequence `proscenium.Chain`, with its companion as a stable value
    `type Chain[+element] = proscenium.Chain[element]` and `val Chain = proscenium.Chain`: a
    `LazyList` behind an opaque type, forwarded as an alias and a stable `val` for the
    reasons `List` is. Its lazy `keep` and `skip` live in its companion, and the umbrella's
    own `keep` and `skip` are shaped so that a `Chain` receiver still reaches them.

entry .infer   `infer[context]`: summons a contextual value at the inline expansion site
    `transparent inline def infer[context]: context = summonInline[context]`, declared in
    the umbrella so that code written against `import soundness.*` has the same spelling
    as proscenium's. Inside an inline method, the search happens where the method is
    expanded rather than where it is defined; `summon` is the non-inline form.

entry .explainMissingContext   explains every failed implicit search, brought in by `import soundness.*`
    The umbrella's form of frontier's catch-all `frontier.context.explainMissingContext`. It
    is a candidate in every implicit search; when a search fails, the compiler reports a
    rendered tree of the candidates tried, what each one required, and which instances on
    the classpath would satisfy it, in place of its own terse message. When the search would
    succeed without it, it steps aside and contributes nothing, so it never changes which
    instance a program uses.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    summon[Char is Concatenable]   // does not compile
    ```

    It is an old-style `transparent inline implicit def` rather than a `given`, because a
    wildcard import excludes givens and this one must be in scope for anyone who writes
    `import soundness.*`. It is annotated `@scala.annotation.internal.diagnostic`, which
    the Proscala compiler honours; frontier's page explains its bounds and the shapes of the
    tree.

entry .repackage   the `@main` entry point of burdock's repackager: `java -cp app.jar soundness.repackage`
    A command-line entry point, in the `soundness` package so that it can be launched by a
    fully-qualified name which every Soundness application carries:

    ```sh
    java -cp app.jar:burdock.jar soundness.repackage --github owner/repo
    ```

    It passes its arguments, as a `List[Text]`, to `burdock.repackage`, which rewrites the
    running application's JAR in place, externalizing its published dependencies; burdock's
    page describes the arguments and the output.

entry .assign   the `@assign(layer)` annotation of stratiform, forwarded as a type alone
    `type assign = stratiform.assign`: a field annotation which places a case-class field in
    a TEL schema layer, as stratiform's page describes it. It is a type alias rather than an
    `export`, since exporting the class would also export its constructor proxy, a term
    `assign`, which would hide anamnesis's `assign` method; the annotation `@assign(…)` reads
    the same either way.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    object withPhone extends Tel.Layer("with-phone")

    case class Contact(name: Text, @assign(withPhone) phone: Optional[Text])
    ```

entry .attribute   the `@attribute` annotation of xylophone, as a type; the term is galilei's
    `type attribute = xylophone.Xml.attribute`: the annotation which marks a case-class field
    as an XML attribute rather than a child element. It is a type alias rather than an
    `export`, because galilei and tarantula already export a *term* named `attribute` into
    the umbrella (reading an extended attribute of a path, or an element's attribute), which
    the case class's companion would collide with; an annotation needs only the type.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Book(@attribute isbn: Text, title: Text)
    ```

entry .thru   `start thru end`: the interval between two ordinals, end inclusive, confined if both ends are
    `Ter thru Sen` is the interval of `Ter`, `Quat`, `Quin` and `Sen`. Two overloads, both
    declared in the umbrella: over plain `Ordinal`s the result is a plain `Interval`, and over
    two ordinals confined to the same value (`Ordinal in form`) it is an `Interval in form`,
    whose every ordinal reads that value totally. They are declared here rather than
    exported from denominative because an `export` forwards only one overload, and a plain
    `Ordinal` receiver would then be offered the confined one and fail.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    (Sec thru Ter).tabulate(_.n1)          // Sequence(2, 3)
    ```

entry .till   `start till end`: the interval between two ordinals, end exclusive, confined if both ends are
    `Ter till Sen` is `Ter`, `Quat` and `Quin`; `Sec till Sec` is empty. As with `thru`, the
    two overloads — plain and confined — are declared together in the umbrella, because an
    `export` would forward only one.

entry .tabulate   `xs.tabulate { i => … }`: a `Sequence` of one element per ordinal of a value or interval
    The collecting counterpart of `each`: builds a `Sequence` with one element per position,
    in order. Three overloads, declared together in the umbrella for the reason `thru` is:
    over a `Countable` value the lambda receives `Ordinal in value.type`, so its reads of
    the value are bare; over a plain `Interval` it receives a plain `Ordinal`; over an
    `Interval in form`, an `Ordinal in form`. Each delegates to `denominative.tabulate`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val xs = Sequence(10, 20, 30)
    xs.tabulate { i => xs(i) + i.n0 }   // Sequence(10, 21, 32)
    ```

entry .each   `interval.each { i => … }`: visits every ordinal of an interval, in order
    The umbrella declares the two interval overloads of `each` beside the collection `each`
    it exports from rudiments: over a plain `Interval` the lambda receives an `Ordinal`, and
    over a confined `Interval in form` it receives `Ordinal in form`, so a read inside is
    total. All of them must share one scope — two top-level definitions of one name in
    `package soundness` do not overload, and one is silently dropped — and the confined
    overload must sit beside the plain one, or the plain one wins for a confined receiver
    and strips its brand without an error. The collection form, `xs.each { x => … }`, is
    documented on rudiments' page.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val xs = Sequence(1, 2, 3)
    xs.extent.each { i => assert(xs(i) > 0) }
    ```

entry .apply   `xs(index)`: the element at an index, bare for a confined index and `Optional` otherwise
    The universal deindexing read for any value with an `Applicable` instance — the
    collections by `Ordinal`, a `Map` by key, a `Text` by `Ordinal` — re-declared here
    rather than exported, since its evidence is a dependent leading `using` clause which a
    forwarder cannot carry. It delegates inline to `rudiments.apply`, so an index typed
    `Operand in xs.type` (from `confine`, `pick`, `each`, …) reads the element bare and any
    other index is checked, giving an `Optional`. A second overload reads a frozen array typed
    `& Sized[n]` with a literal ordinal, checked against `n` as the code compiles. Rudiments'
    page has the full account.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val xs = Sequence(10, 20, 30)
    xs(Sec)                             // Optional[Int]: 20
    xs.confine(Sec).let { i => xs(i) }  // the read bare inside
    ```

entry .at   `xs.at(index)`: the same read as `xs(index)`, bare for a confined index and `Optional` otherwise
    Identical to `apply`, re-declared in the umbrella with it and delegating to
    `rudiments.at`; kept for code which spells positional access `xs.at(Sec)` and keyed
    access `map.at(key)`.

entry .defines   `xs.defines(index)`: whether an index or key is defined for a value
    True when the value's `Applicable` instance contains the index: an ordinal within a
    collection's extent, or a key present in a `Map`. Re-declared in the umbrella with the
    rest of the deindexing group, with the one-line body duplicated rather than delegated.

entry .confine   `xs.confine(index)`: checks an index against a value and returns it branded to that value
    `Unset` if the index is not defined for the value, and otherwise the index itself typed
    `Operand in xs.type`, so that a following `xs(i)` is bare and checks nothing again.
    Works for keys as well as ordinals. Re-declared in the umbrella with the rest of the
    deindexing group.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val ages = Map(t"Hamlet" -> 30)
    ages.confine(t"Hamlet").let(ages(_))   // 30
    ```

entry .pick   `xs.pick(index) { i => … }`: checks an index and runs a lambda with it confined, or gives `Unset`
    The fused form of `confine` and `let`: the lambda receives the index typed `Operand in
    xs.type`, so its reads of `xs` are bare, and the result is `Optional`, `Unset` when the
    index is not defined. Re-declared in the umbrella with the rest of the deindexing group.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val xs = Sequence(10, 20, 30)
    xs.pick(Ter) { i => xs(i) + i.n0 }   // 32
    ```

entry .unsafeAttested   brands an index, or an interval, as valid for a value on the caller's word
    `xs.unsafeAttested(index)` is `confine` without the check, minting `Operand in xs.type`
    directly; `xs.unsafeAttested(index) { i => … }` scopes the attestation to a lambda; and
    `interval.unsafeAttested(xs)` brands an `Interval` as `Interval in xs.type`. Every form
    takes an erased `Unsafe` token, so the call sits inside `unsafely`, and every call carries
    a comment naming the construction which proves the bound. Re-declared in the umbrella
    with the rest of the deindexing group.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val xs: Sequence[Int] = Sequence(1, 2, 4)

    unsafely:
      // In bounds by construction: the literal above has three elements.
      xs.unsafeAttested(Sec) { i => xs(i) }   // 2
    ```

entry .define   `xs.define(index, value)`: a copy of a container with one position or key set
    Through the receiver's `Definable` instance: `xs.define(Sec, 9)` on a `Sequence`,
    `map.define(key, value)` on a `Map`. Out-of-range ordinals leave the container
    unchanged, and an absent key adds an entry. Re-declared in the umbrella because its
    evidence is a dependent leading `using` clause.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    Map(t"Hamlet" -> 30).define(t"Ophelia", 25)
    ```

entry .omit   `xs.omit(index)`: a copy of a container without one key or position
    Through the receiver's `Omissible` instance: `map.omit(key)` removes an entry, and an
    absent key leaves the map as it was. Re-declared in the umbrella because its evidence is
    a dependent leading `using` clause.

entry .keep   the first (or, with `Bidi.Rtl`, the last) `count` elements, or the leading run matching a predicate
    `xs.keep(3)` takes three elements, `xs.keep(3, Bidi.Rtl)` the last three, and
    `xs.keep(_ < 4)` the leading run that matches — a prefix, not a filter. Shape-preserving
    through `Segmentable`, so a `Text` stays a `Text`. Re-declared in the umbrella rather
    than exported so that a `Chain` receiver, which has no `Segmentable` instance, discards
    this candidate and reaches the lazy `keep` in `Chain`'s companion; a forwarder would
    claim the call and fail.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    t"Hello".keep(1, Bidi.Rtl)         // t"o"
    t"HELLOworld".keep(_.isUpper)      // t"HELLO"
    ```

entry .skip   all but the first (or, with `Bidi.Rtl`, the last) `count` elements, or all after the leading matching run
    The complement of `keep`: `xs.skip(2)` drops two elements, `xs.skip(2, Bidi.Rtl)` the
    last two, and `xs.skip(_ == ' ')` the leading run of spaces. Re-declared in the umbrella
    for the reason `keep` is, so that a `Chain` reaches its companion's lazy `skip`.

entry .via   `stream.via(stage)`: a stream pulled through a stage with a `Ductile` instance
    The umbrella's hand-written forwarder for zephyrine's pull-composition: consumes the
    stream and the stage, and gives a stream of the stage's `Result` type over its
    `Transport`, needing a `Buffering`. A synthesized forwarder would lose the dependent
    `ductile.Result` refinement under capture checking, so this one is written out and
    delegates to `zephyrine.via`; it behaves identically. The *type* `via` (a value computed
    in a locale) is cosmopolite's, in the type namespace, and does not clash.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import codepages.utf8Codepage
    import charsets.utf8Charset

    val bytes: Data = t"The quick brown fox".in[Data]
    bytes.stream.via(summon[Charset]).memoize
    ```

entry .accepting   `intake.accepting(stage)`: an intake pushing through a stage with a `Ductile` instance
    The umbrella's hand-written forwarder for zephyrine's push-composition: consumes the
    intake and the stage, and gives an intake of the stage's `Operand` type over its
    `Upstream`, needing a `Buffering`. Written out rather than exported for the reason `via`
    is, and delegating to `zephyrine.accepting`.

entry .dispose   `handle.dispose()`: releases a WIT resource handle, in Wasm-compiled code
    An inline extension on `xenophile.Wasm.Handle`, in xenophile's `wasm` component only. It
    expands to `WasmInvoke.dispose`, which emits the resource's `[resource-drop]` Component
    Model import; like `invoke`, it is plain `inline` so the import materializes at the
    Wasm-linked call site downstream. A program should dispose every handle it obtains when
    it is done with it, typically in a `finally`.

entry .threads   the threading models of parasite, each a `Threading` given to import by name
    An object of forwarders to `parasite.threads`: `platformThreads`, `virtualThreads`,
    `adaptiveThreads`, `pooledThreads` and `javascriptThreads`, documented on parasite's
    page. It is an object, not a package, as parasite's own is: its givens are
    capabilities, which must live in an object.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import threads.virtualThreads
    ```

entry .supervisors   parasite's global supervisor, as a given to import by name
    An object forwarding `parasite.supervisors.globalSupervisor`, the `PlatformSupervisor`
    as a `Supervisor` given, for code which needs a supervisor directly rather than a
    `Threading`.

entry .unsupervised   parasite's process-lifetime monitor, for blocking outside any `supervise` scope
    An object forwarding `parasite.unsupervised.orphanMonitor`, a `Monitor` which parks the
    calling platform thread and supervises nothing: a deliberate escape hatch for daemon
    lifecycles and process-lifetime waits. Prefer `supervise` wherever the blocking region
    is already scoped.

entry .strategies   the error-handling strategies, merged from every library; `throwUnsafely` is declared here
  detail
      The umbrella's `strategies` choice package. Its givens are `Tactic`s, which decide
      what happens when an error is raised, and each is imported by name, since `import
      strategies.*` does not import givens. Most are forwarders to
      `contingency.strategies` (`fatalErrors`, `mitigation`, `throwSafely`,
      `uncheckedErrors`), documented on contingency's page; `throwUnsafely` alone is
      declared here.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"42".as[Int]
      ```
  refer contingency.strategies
  entry .throwUnsafely   throws every error as an ordinary exception, with no capability required
      A pure `ThrowTactic[Hazard]^{}` delegating to `contingency.strategies.throwUnsafely`,
      so by contravariance it satisfies every `Tactic[error]` at once. It is a delegating
      given rather than an export forwarder: a forwarder is a method, and the umbrella name
      of a value given would land in the capture set of every `^{tactic}` instance summoned
      through it, where a method is not a legal capture reference. Suits scripts and the
      outermost layer of a program; a library should state what it raises instead.

entry .httpServers   the HTTP servers of scintillate, each a `Protocolic` given to import by name
  detail
      The umbrella's `httpServers` choice package: which server answers `tcp"8080".serve[Http]`
      is chosen by importing one of these by name. All four are declared here as
      hand-written forwarders to `scintillate.httpServers`, because a synthesized export
      forwarder loses the givens' capture-annotated refinement types. Each is typed
      `scintillate.httpServers.HttpdFor[port]` for a port of 80, 443, 8080 or 8000, captures
      the `Tactic[Httpd.Error]` and `Monitor` it is given, and needs a `Probate`, an
      `Httpd.Event is Loggable` and a `WebserverErrorPage` in scope.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import probates.awaitProbate
      import webserverErrorPages.minimalErrorPage
      import httpServers.soundnessHttpd

      def main(): Unit = supervise:
        tcp"8080".serve[Http](Http.Response(Http.Ok)(t"served"))
      ```
  refer scintillate.httpServers
  refer scintillate.Httpd
  refer scintillate.SocketServer
  entry .jdkHttpserver   `Http` served by the JDK's `Httpd` on `localhost`
      Forwards `scintillate.httpServers.jdkHttpserver`: the server built on the JDK's
      `com.sun.net.httpserver`, bound to the loopback interface only.
  entry .jdkHttpserverPublic   `Http` served by the JDK's `Httpd` on every interface
      Forwards `scintillate.httpServers.jdkHttpserverPublic`: as `jdkHttpserver`, but bound
      to every network interface, so reachable from other machines.
  entry .soundnessHttpd   `Http` served by scintillate's own `SocketServer` on `localhost`
      Forwards `scintillate.httpServers.soundnessHttpd`: Soundness's own server, which also
      speaks HTTP/2 over TLS, bound to the loopback interface only.
  entry .soundnessHttpdPublic   `Http` served by scintillate's own `SocketServer` on every interface
      Forwards `scintillate.httpServers.soundnessHttpdPublic`: as `soundnessHttpd`, but bound
      to every network interface.
